# How TALOS Works

This page gives a little more detail on how I built TALOS and how the main gait measurements are calculated. The goal is to make the analysis easier to follow without having to read through the entire `app.R` file.

## Input Data

TALOS uses processed IMU recordings together with the metadata file for each trial.

The recordings I used contain sensors at four locations:

- Head
- Lower back
- Left foot
- Right foot

The metadata contains information about the trial as well as the annotated left and right gait events and the boundaries of the U-turn.

The recordings are sampled at 100 Hz.

## Reconstructing Gait Cycles

The gait-event annotations are provided as pairs of sample numbers marking the beginning and end of each swing phase.

I use these annotations to reconstruct the timing of each gait cycle separately for the left and right foot.

For each side:

**Swing time** is the time between the start and end of an annotated swing.

`Swing = Swing End - Swing Start`

**Stance time** is the time between the end of the current swing and the beginning of the next swing.

`Stance = Next Swing Start - Current Swing End`

**Stride time** is the time between the beginning of two consecutive swings on the same side.

`Stride = Next Swing Start - Current Swing Start`

The sample differences are divided by the sampling frequency to convert them to seconds.

## Handling the U-Turn

The walking trials include a straight walk, a 180° U-turn, and a straight walk back.

Because turning can change gait timing, I did not want a gait cycle beginning before the turn and ending after it to be treated as a normal straight-walking stride.

TALOS therefore uses the U-turn boundaries in the metadata to separate the two straight sections. Gait cycles are calculated within each straight section rather than across the turn.

## Asymmetry

After calculating the average timing for each side, TALOS compares the left and right values using:

`Asymmetry (%) = 100 × |L - R| / ((|L| + |R|) / 2)`

I use this for stride, swing, and stance duration.

The asymmetry values are descriptive. TALOS does not use a cutoff to classify a gait as normal or abnormal.

## Gait Variability

I also wanted to look at how consistent the individual gait cycles were within a trial rather than only comparing their averages.

TALOS calculates the coefficient of variation (CV) for stride, swing, and stance duration:

`CV (%) = SD / Mean × 100`

This is calculated separately for the left and right sides.

## IMU Visualization

TALOS displays the gait events with the foot angular-velocity signals so that the event timing can be visually checked against the movement signal.

It also displays synchronized IMU signals from the head, lower back, left foot, and right foot on the same time axis. This makes it easier to look at how movement changes across the different sensor locations during the trial.

## Quality Control

I added input checks because I wanted TALOS to fail clearly when a trial was formatted incorrectly instead of silently producing results.

The program checks things such as required metadata, gait-event availability, sampling frequency, expected sensor signals, event boundaries, and compatibility between multipart files.

A trial passing these checks means that TALOS was able to process the input as expected. It does not mean that the trial has been clinically validated.

## Current Scope

TALOS currently uses the gait-event annotations that come with the dataset rather than detecting gait events directly from raw IMU signals.

I manually tested the program using recordings from ACL injury, knee osteoarthritis, hip osteoarthritis, and healthy participants. This was software testing to make sure the pipeline behaved as expected across different trials, not clinical validation.

Future versions could add automatic gait-event detection and larger cohort-level analyses.
